 "gap": 0.26,
 "music": "bed",
 "title": "Deadlock: who wins?",
 "sources": [
  "PostgreSQL docs, 13.3.4 Deadlocks (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html, fetched 2026-11-24): deadlocks are when 'two (or more) transactions each hold locks that the other wants'; row-level example with two account rows; 'PostgreSQL automatically detects deadlock situations and resolves them by aborting one of the transactions involved'; 'Exactly which transaction will be aborted is difficult to predict and should not be relied upon'; best defense: 'acquire locks on multiple objects in a consistent order'; 'deadlocks can be handled on-the-fly by retrying transactions that abort due to deadlocks'.",
  "Illustration only: rows A and B stand in for the docs' accounts 11111 and 22222."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Two transactions each lock one row, then each wants the other's row. Who wins: the first, the second, or nobody?",
   "caps": "EACH LOCKS *ONE ROW | WANTS *THE OTHER | WHO *WINS?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Transaction one locks row A, and transaction two locks row B.",
   "caps": "T1 LOCKS *A | T2 LOCKS *B"
  },
  {
   "text": "Now one asks for B, and two asks for A. Both wait, forever.",
   "caps": "T1 WANTS *B | T2 WANTS *A | *FOREVER"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's a deadlock: each one holds what the other needs.",
   "caps": "A *DEADLOCK | EACH *HOLDS | WHAT THE OTHER *NEEDS"
  },
  {
   "text": "Answer: nobody wins cleanly. PostgreSQL spots the cycle and aborts one of them.",
   "caps": "*NOBODY WINS | SPOTS THE *CYCLE | *ABORTS ONE"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: you can't predict which one gets killed.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | *WHICH ONE? | *UNPREDICTABLE"
  },
  {
   "text": "The fix is boring: always lock rows in the same order, A before B, everywhere.",
   "caps": "SAME *ORDER | *A BEFORE B | *EVERYWHERE"
  },
  {
   "text": "And retry the one that gets aborted. Did you guess nobody?",
   "caps": "*RETRY | THE *ABORTED ONE | DID YOU GUESS *NOBODY?"
  }
 ],
